<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Backup / transfer rules for API 31+ (android:dataExtractionRules). This is the
   operative file for this app, since minSdk is 31; backup_rules.xml mirrors it.

   pipette_secrets holds the Ed25519 device signing identity and the Hugging Face
   token, and is excluded from BOTH channels for two independent reasons:

   1. They are encrypted under an Android Keystore key, and Keystore material never
      leaves the device. Restoring the ciphertext elsewhere would produce an install
      that reports itself registered while every signed request fails, because
      nothing there can decrypt it.
   2. The signing key IS the device's identity to the management server. A backup
      restores onto a device the operator did not choose and cannot see, so it can
      leave two physical devices holding one client_id, which corrupts the
      per-device attribution the benchmark results exist to provide.

   Reason 2 is about backup, not about custody. Carrying an identity to replacement
   hardware IS supported, through the `auth-export` / `auth-import` headless verbs
   (see docs/pipette-android/architecture.md). That path is deliberately the
   opposite of a backup in every way that matters here: an operator runs it over
   adb, one device at a time, naming the destination, and `auth-import` refuses over
   a signing key that is not the bundle's unless it is forced. So the identity is
   recoverable by someone who means it, and never by a restore nobody supervised.

   registration.json goes with it, and must: it is the other half of the same
   identity. Excluding only the secrets would restore an install that reports itself
   registered (LocalStorage.isRegistered reads that file) while holding no key to
   sign with, so every submission would fail with "No private signing key" and no
   visible cause. Dropping both together makes a restored install present as
   unregistered, which is recoverable in one step from Settings.

   device-transfer is excluded as well as cloud-backup: a direct device-to-device
   transfer moves the same files, so leaving them in would reintroduce every problem
   above on the path users are most likely to take when replacing a phone.

   Job manifests and results are deliberately NOT excluded: they are ordinary app
   data, carry no credential, and are worth keeping across a restore.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="pipette_secrets.xml"/>
        <exclude domain="file" path="Pipette/metadata/registration.json"/>
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="pipette_secrets.xml"/>
        <exclude domain="file" path="Pipette/metadata/registration.json"/>
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
